      "message": "So I thought about this some more, and another option that might be feasible and be a bit more ergonomic to use:\n\nIn `ObjectType.h`:\n```\ntemplate \u003ctypename WGPUHandle\u003e\ninline ObjectType ObjectTypeOf;\n\ntemplate \u003c\u003e\nconstexpr inline ObjectType ObjectTypeOf\u003cWGPUBuffer\u003e \u003d ObjectType::Buffer;\n...\n```\n\nIn `Replayer.h`:\n```\ntemplate \u003ctypename WGPUHandle\u003e\nbool GetFromId(ObjectId, id, WGPUHandle* out) {\n    const auto\u0026 map \u003d mObjectIdMaps[ObjectTypeOf\u003cWGPUHandle\u003e];\n    auto result \u003d map.find(id);\n    if (result \u003d\u003d map.end()) {\n        return false;\n    }\n    *out \u003d reinterpret_cast\u003cWGPUHandle\u003e(result-\u003esecond);\n    return true;\n}\n\ntemplate \u003ctypename WGPUHandle\u003e\nbool SetId(WGPUHandle object, ObjectId id) {\n    mObjectIdMaps[ObjectTypeOf\u003cWGPUHandle\u003e].emplace(id, object);\n    return true;\n}\n```\n\nThen when you use it, I think you can let deduction figure out which one to call, i.e.\n```\nWGPUDevice device \u003d nullptr;\nplayback-\u003eGetFromId(deviceId, \u0026device);\n```\n\nThen in the future, when you have stuff autogenerated, you could probably just generate the mapping between `WGPUHandle` and the actual enum value. You might need to wrap the mapping in a struct though, not entirely sure the rules for that offhand.",
